select public.fd_ai_upsert_chunks('fd-textbook', '825773c58568', 18, $aim_json$[{"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.0#1", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.0", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.0", "heading": "Decide first", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 1, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.0 Decide first", "body": "⚙ Decision — choose first; the theory follows\nThe price of the missing half\nAn ordinary six-month call on Northwind shares with a strike of $30 costs $2.55. A dealer quotes $1.92 for a call with the same terms that ceases to exist if the share price ever falls to $25. What should a call with the same terms that comes into existence only if the share price falls to $25 cost?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.1#2", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.1", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.1", "heading": "Exotic options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 2, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.1 Exotic options", "body": "The calls and puts of the preceding chapters, European or American, are called plain vanilla options. Their properties are standard, and they trade on exchanges or are quoted by dealers in large volume. Derivatives whose payoffs are more complex are called exotic options, or exotics. Most are traded over the counter, and many are created by banks to meet the needs of a particular client. They are used for three reasons. A client's exposure may be better matched by an exotic than by a vanilla option: a company that buys raw materials every week is exposed to the average price, not the price on one date. An exotic may cost less than the vanilla option that would serve nearly as well. And an exotic may express a view, about the path of a price or about volatility, that vanilla options cannot express cheaply.\nSeveral types are simple variations on vanilla options.\nA package is a portfolio of vanilla options, forwards, cash, and the underlying asset, such as the spreads and combinations of Chapter 9. A package is often arranged to cost nothing at the start, as a range forward does."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.1#3", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.1", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.1", "heading": "Exotic options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 3, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.1 Exotic options", "body": "A Bermudan option may be exercised early, but only on specified dates. Its value lies between those of the corresponding European and American options.\nA forward start option begins at a future date, with a strike set at that date equal to the price of the asset then. Options granted to employees are often of this kind. For an asset that pays no dividend, a forward start option at the money has the same value as an option at the money today with the same life.\nA compound option is an option on an option: a call on a call, for example, gives the right to buy a call at a stated price on a stated date. It is a cheap way to secure the right to protection that may not be needed.\nA chooser option gives its holder the right to decide, at a stated date \\(t_1\\), whether the option is a call or a put. It suits an investor who expects a large movement in the price, perhaps on the announcement of the result of a lawsuit, but does not know its direction.\nResult 18.1 — The value of a chooser option"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.1#4", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.1", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.1", "heading": "Exotic options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 4, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.1 Exotic options", "body": "If the call and the put that the holder may choose are European, with strike \\(K\\) and expiration \\(T\\), and the choice is made at time \\(t_1\\), the chooser option on an asset that pays no income is a package of a call with strike \\(K\\) and expiration \\(T\\) and a put with strike \\(Ke^{-r(T-t_1)}\\) and expiration \\(t_1\\): \\[ \\text{chooser}=c(K,T)+p\\!\\left(Ke^{-r(T-t_1)},t_1\\right). \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.1#5", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.1", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.1", "heading": "Exotic options", "kind": "example", "ord": 5, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.1 Exotic options", "body": "Worked example 18.1 — A chooser option\nAn investor expects a court ruling that will move Northwind's share price sharply in three months. Value a chooser option with a strike of $30, expiring in six months, with the choice made in three months. The share price is $28, the volatility 39 percent, and the risk-free rate 5 percent.\n1\nThe call. The six-month call with a strike of $30 is worth $2.55 by the Black–Scholes–Merton formula.\n2\nThe put. Its strike is \\(30e^{-0.05\\times0.25}=\\$29.63\\) and its life three months. Its value is $2.91.\n3\nThe chooser. \\(2.55+2.91=\\$5.46\\). A straddle, which holds both the call and the put for six months, costs \\(2.55+3.81=\\$6.36\\). The chooser is cheaper because its holder must give up one of the two options after three months."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.2#6", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.2", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.2", "heading": "Barrier options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 6, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.2 Barrier options", "body": "A barrier option is an option whose existence depends on whether the price of the underlying asset reaches a specified level, the barrier \\(H\\), during a specified period. A knock-out option ceases to exist when the price reaches the barrier. A knock-in option comes into existence only when the price reaches the barrier. Each may be a call or a put, and the barrier may lie below the current price, a down option, or above it, an up option. There are therefore eight standard types: a down-and-out call, which ceases to exist if the price falls to the barrier, a down-and-in call, an up-and-out call, and so on. Some barrier options pay a rebate, a fixed amount paid when an option is knocked out.\nBarrier options are popular because they are cheaper than vanilla options. A buyer of a down-and-out call who is confident that the price will not fall to the barrier obtains, in that event, the payoff of a vanilla call at a lower price. Since one of a knock-out option and the corresponding knock-in option exists at expiration, and the other does not, the two together give the payoff of the vanilla option in every case.\nResult 18.2 — Parity between knock-in and knock-out options"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.2#7", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.2", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.2", "heading": "Barrier options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 7, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.2 Barrier options", "body": "With the same strike, barrier, and expiration, and no rebate, \\[ \\text{knock-in option}+\\text{knock-out option}=\\text{vanilla option}. \\]\nThis answers the opening question. The down-and-in call is worth \\(2.55-1.92=\\$0.63\\). The figure $4.47 adds the two prices instead of taking their difference, and $1.92 assumes that the knock-in and knock-out options are worth the same. They are not, because a fall from $28 to $25 within six months is not very likely: the knock-out option usually survives, and the knock-in option usually never begins.\nEuropean barrier options in the model of Chapter 11 have formulas. The formula for the down-and-in call with \\(H\\le K\\) shows their character.\nResult 18.3 — The down-and-in call"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.2#8", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.2", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.2", "heading": "Barrier options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 8, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.2 Barrier options", "body": "For a barrier \\(H\\) at or below the strike \\(K\\), continuously monitored, on an asset with income at rate \\(q\\), \\[ c_{\\text{di}}=S_0e^{-qT}\\left(\\frac{H}{S_0}\\right)^{2\\lambda}N(y)-Ke^{-rT}\\left(\\frac{H}{S_0}\\right)^{2\\lambda-2}N\\!\\left(y-\\sigma\\sqrt T\\right), \\] where \\[ \\lambda=\\frac{r-q+\\sigma^{2}/2}{\\sigma^{2}},\\qquad y=\\frac{\\ln\\!\\left[H^{2}/(S_0K)\\right]}{\\sigma\\sqrt T}+\\lambda\\sigma\\sqrt T . \\] The down-and-out call is \\(c-c_{\\text{di}}\\). Formulas for the other types are of the same form."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.2#9", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.2", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.2", "heading": "Barrier options", "kind": "example", "ord": 9, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.2 Barrier options", "body": "Worked example 18.2 — A down-and-out call\nValue a six-month down-and-out call on Northwind shares with a strike of $30 and a barrier of $25, using the data of Worked example 18.1.\n1\nParameters. \\(\\lambda=(0.05+0.07605)/0.1521=0.8287\\); \\(\\sigma\\sqrt T=0.2758\\); \\(y=\\ln[625/840]/0.2758+0.8287\\times0.2758=-1.0721+0.2285=-0.8435\\).\n2\nDown-and-in call. \\((25/28)^{1.6575}=0.8287\\) and \\((25/28)^{-0.3425}=1.0396\\). \\(c_{\\text{di}}=28\\times0.8287\\times N(-0.8435)-30e^{-0.025}\\times1.0396\\times N(-1.1193)=28\\times0.8287\\times0.1995-29.259\\times1.0396\\times0.1315=4.629-4.000=\\$0.63\\).\n3\nDown-and-out call. \\(2.55-0.63=\\$1.92\\), about three quarters of the price of the vanilla call."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.2#10", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.2", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.2", "heading": "Barrier options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 10, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.2 Barrier options", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 18.1 — The value of a barrier call against the barrier\nHow much is saved by accepting a barrier\nThe figure shows the value of a six-month call on Northwind shares with a strike of $30, as a function of the barrier, for knock-out and knock-in options, with the vanilla call as a horizontal line. Select a down or an up barrier, and vary the volatility. At every barrier the two curves sum to the vanilla line."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.2#11", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.2", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.2", "heading": "Barrier options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 11, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.2 Barrier options", "body": "An up-and-out call is worth much less than a down-and-out call with a barrier equally far from the current price, because it is knocked out in precisely the states in which the call pays most. With a barrier of $40 the up-and-out call is worth only $0.66, about a quarter of the vanilla price. Its value as a function of the share price and the time to expiration, shown in Interactive figure 18.2, has a form unlike that of any vanilla option: it rises with the share price at first, reaches a maximum, and falls to zero at the barrier."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.2#12", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.2", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.2", "heading": "Barrier options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 12, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.2 Barrier options", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 18.2 — Three-dimensional view\nThe value surface of an up-and-out call\nThe surface is the value of an up-and-out call on Northwind shares with a strike of $30 and a barrier of $40, for share prices from $15 to the barrier and times to expiration up to one year, with a volatility of 39 percent. Select the value or the delta. The gold curve is the selected time to expiration and the marker the selected share price. Drag to rotate; scroll or pinch to zoom.\nTable equivalent of this figure"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.2#13", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.2", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.2", "heading": "Barrier options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 13, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.2 Barrier options", "body": "The surface explains why barrier options are difficult to hedge. Near the barrier and close to expiration, the value falls from several dollars to zero over a small range of share prices. Delta is then large and negative, and gamma is very large; a dealer who has sold the option must trade heavily as the price approaches the barrier, and the trades themselves may push the price through it."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.2#14", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.2", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.2", "heading": "Barrier options", "kind": "warning", "ord": 14, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.2 Barrier options", "body": "Common error\nThe formulas assume that the barrier is monitored continuously. Most contracts observe the price once a day or once a week, and an option observed less often is knocked out less often. Treating a discretely monitored option as continuous undervalues a knock-out option and overvalues a knock-in option. A correction due to Mark Broadie, Paul Glasserman, and Steven Kou moves the barrier away from the current price by the factor \\(e^{0.5826\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}}\\), where \\(\\Delta t\\) is the interval between observations, and applies the continuous formula. For the down-and-out call of Worked example 18.2 with weekly observation, the barrier falls from $25 to \\(25e^{-0.5826\\times0.39\\times\\sqrt{1/52}}=\\$24.22\\), and the value rises from $1.92 to $2.16."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.3#15", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.3", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.3", "heading": "Binary options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 15, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.3 Binary options", "body": "A binary option, or digital option, has a discontinuous payoff. A cash-or-nothing call pays a fixed amount \\(Q\\) if the price of the asset at expiration is above the strike, and nothing otherwise. An asset-or-nothing call pays the price of the asset itself if it ends above the strike, and nothing otherwise. Puts are defined in the same way for prices below the strike. In a risk-neutral world the probability that the price ends above the strike is \\(N(d_2)\\), as Section 11.6 showed, which gives the value of the first. The value of the second follows from a change of measure, derived in Section 18.7.\nResult 18.4 — Binary options\nThe value of a cash-or-nothing call paying \\(Q\\) is \\(Qe^{-rT}N(d_2)\\), and the value of an asset-or-nothing call is \\(S_0e^{-qT}N(d_1)\\), where \\(d_1\\) and \\(d_2\\) are those of the Black–Scholes–Merton formula. A vanilla call is a long asset-or-nothing call and a short position in \\(K\\) cash-or-nothing calls each paying $1: \\[ c=S_0e^{-qT}N(d_1)-Ke^{-rT}N(d_2). \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.3#16", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.3", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.3", "heading": "Binary options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 16, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.3 Binary options", "body": "The decomposition gives the two terms of the formula of Chapter 11 a direct meaning. The first is the value of receiving the share when the call is exercised, and the second is the value of paying the strike when it is exercised."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.3#17", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.3", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.3", "heading": "Binary options", "kind": "example", "ord": 17, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.3 Binary options", "body": "Worked example 18.3 — Binary options on Northwind\nValue a six-month cash-or-nothing call paying $100 if the price of Northwind shares exceeds $30, and the corresponding asset-or-nothing call, using the data of Worked example 18.1.\n1\nParameters. \\(d_1=[\\ln(28/30)+(0.05+0.07605)\\times0.5]/0.2758=-0.0216\\) and \\(d_2=-0.2974\\), so that \\(N(d_1)=0.4914\\) and \\(N(d_2)=0.3831\\).\n2\nValues. Cash-or-nothing: \\(100e^{-0.025}\\times0.3831=\\$37.36\\). Asset-or-nothing: \\(28\\times0.4914=\\$13.76\\).\n3\nCheck. The vanilla call is \\(13.76-30\\times0.3736=13.76-11.21=\\$2.55\\), in agreement with Worked example 18.1."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.3#18", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.3", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.3", "heading": "Binary options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 18, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.3 Binary options", "body": "Concept check 18.1\nA cash-or-nothing call paying $1 is worth $0.40, and an asset-or-nothing call with the same strike of $50 and the same expiration is worth $23.00. What is the value of the vanilla call with the same terms?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.4#19", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.4", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.4", "heading": "Lookback and Asian options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 19, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.4 Lookback and Asian options", "body": "The payoffs of lookback and Asian options depend on the whole path of the price, and not only on its final value. A floating lookback call pays \\(S_T-S_{\\min}\\), the amount by which the final price exceeds the lowest price during the life of the option: it buys at the lowest price. A floating lookback put pays \\(S_{\\max}-S_T\\): it sells at the highest. A fixed lookback call pays \\(\\max(S_{\\max}-K,0)\\). Lookback options are always exercised, since the final price can be no lower than the minimum, and are therefore expensive. A six-month floating lookback call on Northwind shares is worth $5.93, against $3.39 for a vanilla call at the money."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.4#20", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.4", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.4", "heading": "Lookback and Asian options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 20, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.4 Lookback and Asian options", "body": "An Asian option has a payoff that depends on the average price of the asset over the life of the option. An average price call pays \\(\\max(S_{\\text{ave}}-K,0)\\), and an average strike call pays \\(\\max(S_T-S_{\\text{ave}},0)\\). Asian options are cheaper than vanilla options, because the average of prices over a period varies less than the price at a single date. They are widely used in markets for commodities and currencies, where a company's exposure is to the average price of its purchases or sales over a period. They are also harder to manipulate, since a movement of the price on one day changes the average little. For a six-month average price call on Northwind shares with a strike of $30, based on 26 weekly prices, simulation of the kind described in Chapter 16 gives a value of about $1.18, less than half that of the vanilla call."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.4#21", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.4", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.4", "heading": "Lookback and Asian options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 21, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.4 Lookback and Asian options", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 18.3 — One path, four payoffs\nThe payoffs of exotic options on a simulated path\nThe figure shows a simulated path of the weekly price of Northwind shares over six months, starting at $28, with the strike of $30, the barrier of $25, the running minimum, and the running average. The readout gives the payoff on this path of a vanilla call, an average price call, a floating lookback call, and a down-and-out call, each with a strike of $30 where one applies. Draw new paths and compare the payoffs with the values of the options."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.4#22", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.4", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.4", "heading": "Lookback and Asian options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 22, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.4 Lookback and Asian options", "body": "Concept check 18.2\nSix-month options on the same share, all with a strike of $30 where one applies, are listed below. Which ranking of their values, from highest to lowest, is correct?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.5#23", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.5", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.5", "heading": "Structured products", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 23, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.5 Structured products", "body": "A structured product is a security, usually a note issued by a bank, whose return is linked by a formula to the price of an asset or an index. Structured products are sold to investors, many of them individuals, who could not easily build the position themselves. Each can be decomposed into a bond and a package of options, vanilla or exotic, and the decomposition is the means of valuing it and of judging whether its price is fair.\nA principal-protected note returns the investor's principal at maturity in every case, together with a share of any rise in an index. It is a zero-coupon bond and a call option on the index. The bank invests part of the principal in the bond, which grows to the principal at maturity, and spends the rest on calls. The proportion of the rise in the index that the investor receives is the participation rate."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.5#24", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.5", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.5", "heading": "Structured products", "kind": "example", "ord": 24, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.5 Structured products", "body": "Worked example 18.4 — A principal-protected note\nA bank offers a five-year note of $1,000 that repays the principal and a share of the rise in a stock index. The five-year risk-free rate is 4 percent with continuous compounding, the dividend yield on the index is 2 percent, and its volatility is 20 percent. Find the participation rate if the bank takes no profit.\n1\nThe bond. \\(1{,}000e^{-0.04\\times5}=\\$818.73\\). This sum, invested today, repays the principal in five years.\n2\nThe options. \\(1{,}000-818.73=\\$181.27\\) remains. A five-year call at the money on $1,000 of the index costs $199.26 by the Black–Scholes–Merton formula with \\(q=0.02\\).\n3\nParticipation. \\(181.27/199.26=0.910\\). The investor receives 91 percent of any rise in the index. If the bank prices the note to earn 2 percent of the principal, $20, the participation rate falls to \\(161.27/199.26=81\\) percent. Since the investor also forgoes the dividends on the index and the interest on the principal, the protection is not free."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.5#25", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.5", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.5", "heading": "Structured products", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 25, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.5 Structured products", "body": "The participation rate is higher when interest rates are high, since the bond then costs less, and when volatility is low, since the calls then cost less. It is common to raise the rate by replacing the vanilla call with an exotic one: an Asian call, a call on which the gain is capped, or an up-and-out call. Each is cheaper and pays less in some states. A reverse convertible pays a high coupon, but repays the principal in shares if the share price falls below a strike at maturity. It is a bond and a short put, and its high coupon is the premium of the put that the investor has sold. An autocallable note pays a coupon and is redeemed early if the index is above its initial level on any of a series of observation dates, and exposes the investor to losses, through a down-and-in put, if the index falls below a barrier. Such products have attracted the attention of regulators, because the risks that investors bear are often not apparent from the terms of sale."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.6#26", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.6", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.6", "heading": "Lessons from derivatives losses", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 26, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.6 Lessons from derivatives losses", "body": "The losses listed in Table 18.1 are among the largest in the history of derivatives. Some fell on financial institutions, through trading by their employees; some on nonfinancial companies and local governments, which used derivatives in place of the hedges they intended; and some on funds whose strategies failed in conditions they had not foreseen.\nTable 18.1 — Selected losses from derivatives\nOrganizationYearApproximate lossInstruments and cause\nMetallgesellschaft1993$1.3 billionLong-term fixed-price oil supply contracts hedged with short-term futures rolled forward; funding of margin calls\nOrange County, California1994$1.7 billionLeveraged positions in interest rate products by the county treasurer; rise in interest rates\nProcter & Gamble1994$157 millionHighly leveraged interest rate swaps with Bankers Trust; rise in interest rates\nBarings1995$1.4 billionUnauthorized positions in Nikkei futures and options by a trader in Singapore; concealed losses\nLong-Term Capital Management1998$4.6 billionHighly leveraged convergence trades; flight to quality after the Russian default"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.6#27", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.6", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.6", "heading": "Lessons from derivatives losses", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 27, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.6 Lessons from derivatives losses", "body": "Amaranth Advisors2006$6.6 billionPositions in natural gas futures; adverse movement in spreads between delivery months\nSociété Générale2008$7.2 billionUnauthorized positions in stock index futures by a trader; concealed by fictitious trades\nAmerican International Group2008Tens of billionsProtection sold on senior CDO tranches; collateral demands\nJPMorgan Chase2012$6.2 billionLarge positions in credit indices by its Chief Investment Office in London\nArchegos Capital (losses to its banks)2021Over $10 billionConcentrated share positions held through total return swaps with several banks"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.6#28", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.6", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.6", "heading": "Lessons for all users", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 28, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.6 Lessons for all users", "body": "The first lesson is to define risk limits and enforce them. Every organization that uses derivatives should state clearly the risks that it is prepared to take, measure them daily by the methods of Chapters 13 and 14, and require that limits be observed. Limits are of no value if they are relaxed whenever a trader with a record of profits exceeds them; several of the losses in Table 18.1 grew from positions that had been profitable until they were not.\nThe second is that no one can consistently outguess the market. A trader who has made large profits in the past has not necessarily acquired a superior method; the profits may have come from taking large risks that happened to pay. Treasurers and fund managers who believed they could predict interest rates, the prices of commodities, or the convergence of spreads lost the most."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.6#29", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.6", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.6", "heading": "Lessons for all users", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 29, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.6 Lessons for all users", "body": "The third is not to underestimate the benefits of diversification, and its corollary, to beware of concentration. Amaranth and Archegos each held positions so large in relation to their markets that they could not be closed without moving prices against themselves. The losses of Archegos fell on its banks because each had financed its positions through total return swaps without knowing the size of the positions that it had with the others.\nThe fourth is to carry out scenario analyses and stress tests. Value at risk computed from a few years of normal markets says little about the losses in a crisis, when correlations rise and liquidity disappears. Long-Term Capital Management lost most of its capital in a few weeks of 1998, when spreads that it expected to narrow widened together across many markets."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.6#30", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.6", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.6", "heading": "Lessons for financial institutions", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 30, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.6 Lessons for financial institutions", "body": "Banks should monitor traders carefully and, above all, separate the front, middle, and back offices. Nick Leeson at Barings controlled both the trading desk and the settlement of its trades in Singapore, and could hide his losses in an account that he created for the purpose. Jérôme Kerviel at Société Générale had worked in the middle office and knew how to enter fictitious offsetting trades that escaped its controls. Banks should be cautious about models, especially for products whose prices cannot be checked against the market: a trade that appears very profitable when valued by a model may simply be mispriced by it. They should be conservative in recognizing day-one profits, spreading them over the life of a trade, so that traders are not rewarded for transactions that a model misvalues. They should not sell clients inappropriate products; Bankers Trust paid heavily, in money and reputation, for the swaps it sold to Procter & Gamble and other clients who did not understand them. And they should not ignore liquidity risk: a position whose value is measured at mid-market prices may realize much less when it must be sold in a hurry."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.6#31", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.6", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.6", "heading": "Lessons for nonfinancial companies", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 31, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.6 Lessons for nonfinancial companies", "body": "A company should fully understand the trades it makes. A treasurer who cannot value a derivative, or explain how its value would change in each plausible scenario, should not buy it. Procter & Gamble's swaps paid a formula in which a rise in interest rates was multiplied many times; the company appears not to have understood the leverage until rates rose. A company should ensure that a hedger does not become a speculator. The treasurer of Orange County, Robert Citron, earned high returns for the county for several years by leveraged bets that interest rates would not rise, and was praised for it until 1994. A company should also be cautious about making the treasury department a profit center, since the pursuit of profit leads to the taking of risk. And it should consider the funding of its hedges. Metallgesellschaft's hedge was sound in principle, but when oil prices fell its futures required large margin payments at once, while the gains on its supply contracts would arrive only over ten years. Its parent company closed the hedge and realized the loss."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.6#32", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.6", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.6", "heading": "Lessons for nonfinancial companies", "kind": "notes", "ord": 32, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.6 Lessons for nonfinancial companies", "body": "Historical note\nBarings, founded in 1762, had financed the Louisiana Purchase and was the oldest merchant bank in London. Its trader in Singapore, Nick Leeson, began to hide losses in 1992 in an error account numbered 88888. By early 1995 he had accumulated very large long positions in Nikkei 225 futures and had sold straddles on the index, a bet that it would neither fall nor become volatile. The Kobe earthquake of 17 January 1995 caused the index to fall sharply. Leeson doubled his positions in an attempt to recover. The losses of £827 million exceeded the bank's capital, and in March 1995 Barings was sold to ING for one pound."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.6#33", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.6", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.6", "heading": "Lessons for nonfinancial companies", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 33, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.6 Lessons for nonfinancial companies", "body": "Concept check 18.3\nWhich control would most directly have prevented the concealment of losses at Barings?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.7#34", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.7", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.7", "heading": "★ Derivation of the prices of binary and chooser options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 34, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.7 ★ Derivation of the prices of binary and chooser options", "body": "Optional section. This section derives Result 18.4 from risk-neutral valuation, using a change of numeraire for the asset-or-nothing call, and Result 18.1 from put–call parity.\nCash-or-nothing call. By Principle 10.1 its value is \\(e^{-rT}Q\\,\\Pr(S_T>K)\\), with the probability computed in a risk-neutral world. There \\(\\ln S_T\\) is normal with mean \\(\\ln S_0+(r-q-\\sigma^{2}/2)T\\) and standard deviation \\(\\sigma\\sqrt T\\), so that \\[ \\Pr(S_T>K)=N\\!\\left(\\frac{\\ln(S_0/K)+(r-q-\\sigma^{2}/2)T}{\\sigma\\sqrt T}\\right)=N(d_2), \\] and the value is \\(Qe^{-rT}N(d_2)\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.7#35", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.7", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.7", "heading": "★ Derivation of the prices of binary and chooser options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 35, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.7 ★ Derivation of the prices of binary and chooser options", "body": "Asset-or-nothing call. Its value is \\(e^{-rT}E[S_T\\mathbf 1_{S_T>K}]\\). Write \\(S_T=S_0e^{(r-q-\\sigma^2/2)T+\\sigma\\sqrt T\\varepsilon}\\), with \\(\\varepsilon\\) standard normal. Then \\[ e^{-rT}E[S_T\\mathbf 1_{S_T>K}]=S_0e^{-qT}\\int_{-d_2}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-\\sigma^{2}T/2+\\sigma\\sqrt T x-x^{2}/2}\\,dx=S_0e^{-qT}\\int_{-d_2}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-(x-\\sigma\\sqrt T)^{2}/2}\\,dx . \\] The substitution \\(u=x-\\sigma\\sqrt T\\) gives the integral \\(N(d_2+\\sigma\\sqrt T)=N(d_1)\\), and the value is \\(S_0e^{-qT}N(d_1)\\). In the language of numeraires, \\(N(d_1)\\) is the probability that the call is exercised when the share, rather than the money market account, is the unit of value. Subtracting \\(K\\) cash-or-nothing calls of $1 from the asset-or-nothing call gives the payoff \\(\\max(S_T-K,0)\\) and so the Black–Scholes–Merton formula."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.7#36", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.7", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.7", "heading": "★ Derivation of the prices of binary and chooser options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 36, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.7 ★ Derivation of the prices of binary and chooser options", "body": "Chooser option. At time \\(t_1\\) the holder chooses the more valuable of the call and the put, and the chooser is worth \\(\\max(c_1,p_1)\\), where \\(c_1\\) and \\(p_1\\) are their values then. For an asset with no income, put–call parity at time \\(t_1\\) gives \\(p_1=c_1+Ke^{-r(T-t_1)}-S_{t_1}\\). Hence \\[ \\max(c_1,p_1)=c_1+\\max\\!\\left(0,\\;Ke^{-r(T-t_1)}-S_{t_1}\\right). \\] The first term is the value at \\(t_1\\) of a call with strike \\(K\\) and expiration \\(T\\); the second is the payoff at \\(t_1\\) of a put with strike \\(Ke^{-r(T-t_1)}\\) expiring at \\(t_1\\). Valuing each today gives Result 18.1. With income at rate \\(q\\), the put is on \\(e^{-q(T-t_1)}\\) units of the asset, and its strike is \\(Ke^{-(r-q)(T-t_1)}\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.summary#37", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.summary", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 37, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.summary Summary", "body": "Exotic options are options with nonstandard payoffs, traded over the counter. They match clients' exposures more closely, cost less than vanilla options that would serve, or express views on the path of a price or on volatility.\nA chooser option permits its holder to decide at time \\(t_1\\) whether it is a call or a put; it is a call with strike \\(K\\) and expiration \\(T\\) and a put with strike \\(Ke^{-r(T-t_1)}\\) and expiration \\(t_1\\).\nBarrier options are knocked out or knocked in when the price reaches a barrier. A knock-in and a knock-out option with the same terms sum to the vanilla option. Barrier options are cheaper than vanilla options, difficult to hedge near the barrier, and must be valued with allowance for discrete monitoring.\nA cash-or-nothing call is worth \\(Qe^{-rT}N(d_2)\\) and an asset-or-nothing call \\(S_0e^{-qT}N(d_1)\\). A vanilla call is an asset-or-nothing call less \\(K\\) cash-or-nothing calls.\nLookback options pay according to the maximum or minimum price, are always exercised, and are expensive. Asian options pay according to the average price and are cheaper than vanilla options."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.summary#38", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.summary", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 38, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.summary Summary", "body": "A structured product is a bond combined with options. A principal-protected note is a zero-coupon bond and a call; its participation rate is the money left after buying the bond divided by the price of the call.\nThe largest derivatives losses teach that risk limits must be defined and enforced, trading must be separated from settlement, models and day-one profits must be treated with caution, users must understand the products they trade, hedgers must not become speculators, and the funding and liquidity of positions must be considered."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.exercises#39", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.exercises", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 39, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.exercises Exercises", "body": "18.1 A vanilla European put is worth $4.20. A down-and-out put with the same strike and expiration, and no rebate, is worth $3.10. Find the value of the down-and-in put. What happens to the value of the down-and-out put as the barrier is lowered toward zero?\nBy Result 18.2 the down-and-in put is worth \\(4.20-3.10=\\$1.10\\). As the barrier falls toward zero, the probability of reaching it falls toward zero, and the down-and-out put approaches the vanilla put, $4.20, while the down-and-in put approaches zero.\n18.2 A share that pays no dividend is priced at $50. Find the value of a one-year cash-or-nothing call that pays $1,000 if the share price exceeds $50 at expiration. The risk-free rate is 4 percent and the volatility 25 percent.\n\\(d_2=[\\ln1+(0.04-0.03125)]/0.25=0.035\\) and \\(N(d_2)=0.5140\\). The value is \\(1{,}000e^{-0.04}\\times0.5140=\\$493.81\\).\n18.3 Value a chooser option on a share priced at $50 that pays no dividend, with a strike of $50, expiring in one year, with the choice made in six months. The risk-free rate is 6 percent and the volatility 30 percent."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.exercises#40", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.exercises", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 40, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.exercises Exercises", "body": "The one-year call with a strike of $50 is worth $7.36. The six-month put with a strike of \\(50e^{-0.03}=\\$48.52\\) is worth $2.81. The chooser is worth \\(7.36+2.81=\\$10.17\\).\n18.4 A three-year principal-protected note of $1,000 pays a share of the rise in an index. The three-year risk-free rate is 5 percent with continuous compounding, and a three-year call at the money on $1,000 of the index costs $156.75. Find the participation rate if the bank takes no profit. How would it change if interest rates fell?\nThe bond costs \\(1{,}000e^{-0.15}=\\$860.71\\), leaving $139.29 for calls. The participation rate is \\(139.29/156.75=88.9\\) percent. If interest rates fell, the bond would cost more and leave less for the calls, and the calls themselves would cost slightly less; the first effect dominates, and the participation rate would fall.\n18.5 An up-and-out call has a barrier equal to its strike. What is its value? What is the value of the corresponding up-and-in call? Explain."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.exercises#41", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.exercises", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 41, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.exercises Exercises", "body": "The up-and-out call is worthless: it can pay only if the price ends above the strike, and to do so the price must pass the barrier, which knocks the option out. The up-and-in call therefore equals the vanilla call, by Result 18.2: every path on which the vanilla call pays has reached the barrier.\n18.6 An airline buys jet fuel every day. Explain why an average price call on jet fuel over a quarter may be a better hedge for it than a vanilla call expiring at the end of the quarter, and why it costs less.\nThe airline's cost over the quarter depends on the average price it pays, not on the price on the last day. The average price call pays when the average exceeds the strike, which matches the airline's exposure; a vanilla call might pay nothing although prices were high for most of the quarter and fell at its end, or pay much although they were low until its last days. The average of prices over a period has a lower volatility than the price on one date, so that the average price call is cheaper."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.exercises#42", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.exercises", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 42, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.exercises Exercises", "body": "18.7 A reverse convertible on Northwind shares pays a coupon of 12 percent for one year and repays the principal in cash if the share price at maturity is at least its initial level of $28, and in shares, at $28 each, if it is below. The one-year risk-free rate is 5 percent and a one-year put at the money is worth $3.57. Decompose the note, and explain the size of the coupon.\nThe note is a one-year bond and a short position in a put with a strike of $28 on one share for each $28 of principal: when the price ends below $28, the investor receives shares worth less than the principal, which is the payoff of the short put. The put is worth \\(3.57/28=12.8\\) percent of the principal. The investor receives, in the coupon of 12 percent, about the risk-free rate of 5 percent plus a little over half the value of the put, whose present value per year is about 7 percentage points; the bank keeps the remainder of the premium."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.exercises#43", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.exercises", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 43, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.exercises Exercises", "body": "18.8 Metallgesellschaft had agreed to supply oil at fixed prices for ten years and hedged by buying short-term futures that it rolled forward each month. Explain why the hedge could be sound in economic terms and still lead to the failure of the strategy when oil prices fell.\nA fall in oil prices raised the value of the fixed-price supply contracts, since the company would buy oil more cheaply than the price at which it had agreed to sell it, and lowered the value of the long futures by a similar amount. But the futures were settled daily, so that the losses on them had to be paid in cash at once, while the gains on the supply contracts would be received only as oil was delivered over ten years. The hedge created a severe need for funds. The company's parent, alarmed by the cash outflows, closed the futures positions, realizing the losses on the hedge while keeping the long-dated contracts. The lesson is that a hedge must be judged by its cash flows as well as its values."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:18.further-reading#44", "source_key": "fd-textbook:18.further-reading", "chapter": 18, "section": "18.further-reading", "heading": "Further reading", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 44, "title": "Chapter 18, Exotic Options, Structured Products, and Lessons from Derivatives Losses — 18.further-reading Further reading", "body": "Broadie, M., Glasserman, P., and Kou, S. G. (1997). A continuity correction for discrete barrier options. Mathematical Finance, 7(4), 325–349.\nCulp, C. L., and Miller, M. H. (1995). Metallgesellschaft and the economics of synthetic storage. Journal of Applied Corporate Finance, 7(4), 62–76.\nHull, J. C. (2022). Options, Futures, and Other Derivatives (11th ed.). Pearson. Chapters 26 and 36.\nJorion, P. (2000). Risk management lessons from Long-Term Capital Management. European Financial Management, 6(3), 277–300.\nLowenstein, R. (2000). When Genius Failed: The Rise and Fall of Long-Term Capital Management. Random House.\nRubinstein, M., and Reiner, E. (1991). Breaking down the barriers. Risk, 4(8), 28–35."}]$aim_json$::jsonb, 'Financial Derivatives (MacroNations textbook)');
